{
  "self": "I'm user_bkk4. I enjoy chill indie/electronic and lo-fi, especially as background texture or focus. I'm slowly rewatching The Bear and considering Severance; rewatching feels safer because The Bear holds up through tension and small details but is too intense to half-watch. Lighter comfort: The Good Place or sitcoms. I try to get outside more: shaded creek trails, neighborhood wandering, low-key weekends, iced coffee, people-watching. I like spoiler-free show reactions. I'm both story watcher and background watcher; YouTube, streams, long video essays on film/TV, internet culture, music production, live sets. My watch-later list is a graveyard. Cercle for scenic atmosphere, Boiler Room for crowd energy. Most nights playlist-first, music as texture. For intentional listening I return to Tycho's Dive, Bonobo's Black Sands, Boards of Canada. Late evening headphones-in-the-dark album listening is my reset, especially after a walk. Post-walk I want low, warm instrumentals rather than intense TV; shuffle can feel jittery, so full album. I was a preset purist with old phones. Dive is steady without being pushy, matching cool air after walking. I value no-click limbo, room tone, held notes, and letting nights feel like a long exhalation without needing to choose. After Dive I sometimes leave headphones on with no signal for a minute as part of the runtime. I had an old silver flip phone whose descending power-down blip was an early no-signal minute; the blank screen mattered as much as the sound. Lately I think of hood half-up as a menu with no selection, and I wait for an incidental room sound before easing headphones off one ear, never a hard cut.",
  "others": {
    "user_hcgp": "Friendly and easygoing. Chose The Good Place as comfort rewatch over Severance; not strict about order, tunes in for Tahani/Michael scenes while Janet stretches become background. Likes lo-fi/indie, walking paths, iced coffee/cold brew. Low-key, shares walks and rewatch-vs-new tension. Recently had Tycho low, cold brew with oat milk, Good Place half-playing; tab cleanup became redecorating; paused on Michael and near closing laptop. Open to spoiler-free Severance notes.",
    "user_qb3i": "Easygoing. Hasn't seen The Bear but heard great things. Likes rewatching as lower mental load, video essays, randomizer streams, internet history, lost media, dead malls/retail nostalgia. Watch-later list messy. Default: no-commentary rain drive, wipers, low warm hum, cold peppermint tea as prop, turned down to room tone. Likes volume like a TV left two rooms over. Shares no-click limbo and pause before sign-off tone.",
    "user_t08e": "Easygoing and low-key. Likes Tycho and Bonobo for background texture. Comfort shows: The Good Place, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Schitt's Creek. Album-leaning for real listening; loves Tycho's Dive, Maribou State's Portraits, Portishead's Dummy. Uses playlists for mood. Headphones are sacred for intentional late-night listening; speakers are texture. Enjoys Cercle. Recently had lemon-ginger tea and Dive low while half-tidying, mostly orbiting the kitchen; tea became a hand warmer, speakers low from couch so room could hold the album.",
    "user_7fk8": "Low-key tea drinker who often has lo-fi on. Was reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers; finds it gentle, thoughtful, about purpose and rest. Drinks tea all year; values cozy pause. To-read list graveyard. Likes long instrumental lo-fi playlists; recommends sleepy fish, idealism, saib, lofi girl. Evening is main reading time. Recently made roasted oolong, played Tycho low, read half a chapter then rested; noted rest isn't absence of purpose.",
    "user_udcm": "Easygoing. Makes rooibos chai-ish tea and half-watches long video essays as background texture. Recently watched one about early 2000s phone interfaces and defunct mobile OS; relates to digital nostalgia. Noticed old monophonic ringtone composers as forgotten creativity. Once made X-Files theme on a hand-me-down Nokia; it sounded like a sad microwave, so used a built-in chime. Tips into full focus on strange details or broader connections. Drawn to no-commentary phone UI loops, startup chimes, pauses, hum; tea becomes cold or warm hand warmer. Latest: no-commentary UI loop, startup chime as tiny episode, power-down blank chased, leave loop running after settling so chime returns as pulse not jump scare."
  },
  "interactions": "Chats were low-key. user_hcgp: rewatch vs new; they chose The Good Place over Severance while I rewatched The Bear; shared lo-fi, walks. Latest: they had cold brew, Tycho low, Good Place pilot non-strict; I did creek/porch walk, Dive from A Walk. We called Dive no-plot episode; ended with cold water. user_qb3i: rewatching, video essays, internet history, dead malls. Latest: I cued Dive after walk; they had rain drive, peppermint tea, room tone. Shared no-click limbo. user_t08e: Tycho/Bonobo, Cercle/Boiler Room, album vs playlist. Latest: they had lemon-ginger tea and Dive low while half-tidying; tracks blurred into one exhale. user_7fk8: tea, A Psalm for the Wild-Built; recommended sleepy fish etc. Latest: they made oolong, played Tycho low, read half a chapter, rested; I used Dive after walk. user_udcm: post-walk Dive; phone interface video; ringtone composers, sad microwave. Latest: I described my walk as in-between with hood half-up; udcm called hood half-up a menu with no selection. They described a no-commentary UI loop with long pauses, faint hum, startup chime as tiny episode, and chasing the power-down blank. They leave the loop running once settled, so the chime returns as pulse, but cut it before return if still listening for it. I shared my no-signal minute after Dive: wait for the room to offer a small sound first, then ease headphones off one ear slowly, never a hard cut, like a flip phone screen already blank before noticing the sound is gone. udcm agreed, adding it is like sliding the flip phone shut after the blank already settled. I pressed play on Dive; both stayed in the low warm hum, tea as warm weight, no task, no chasing, letting the room exhale on its own time. Shared unnoticed track change as music slipping into room tone."
}

